   290	### 5.4 Loophole bans
   291	
   292	Each of these is a way a goal could look closed while the system does not meet it. Each is banned, and the
   293	validity rule or closure rule that enforces the ban is named.
   294	
   295	| # | Loophole | Ban | Enforced by |
   296	| --- | --- | --- | --- |
   297	| B1 | **Skips.** A live test skipped because `XTDB_INTEGRATION` or `QLEVER_INTEGRATION` was unset, a case filtered out, or zero tests ran. | A skip is a FAIL. Executed ids must equal declared ids. | V10, CR-PASS-ALL |
   298	| B2 | **Unsupported markers.** "unsupported", "not implemented", a capability flag set to false, or a typed refusal of a shape the obligation requires. | Counts as FAIL. Refusing a required shape never closes an obligation, even when the refusal is correct and typed. | V10, R.native.fragment-equivalence, CR-MATRIX |
   299	| B3 | **Lowered thresholds.** A CALIBRATE value set or later changed to make a run pass. | Thresholds are frozen in a create-only calibration receipt before acceptance, respect the hard floors and ceilings in `load-contract.json`, and may only be tightened. A changed profile is a new named profile; the original remains the acceptance profile and its failures stay. | CR-PERF, CR-LOAD, L.calibration.frozen |
   300	| B4 | **Stale builds.** Typecheck of new source while executing old `dist`, or a dirty tree identified only by a dirty flag. | The receipt binds the full tree including uncommitted changes and the hashes of the `dist` files actually imported, built in the same run. | V2, V3 |
   301	| B5 | **Wrong endpoints.** A run against the homebase (15442, 17019), another slot, or an engine started from a different image. | The contacted endpoint must equal the slot's; the image digest must equal the pin. | V5, V6 |
   302	| B6 | **Hybrid-language tasks counted for one language.** A task whose driver is SQL and final read XTQL counted as XTQL. | A hybrid task counts for neither XTDB language; host steps that touch data make a task hybrid. | V11, PKT.full142 |
   303	| B7 | **Calibration runs as acceptance.** | `runKind` must be `acceptance`; calibration, diagnostic, probe and author-preview runs close nothing. | V12 |
   304	| B8 | **Inherited homebase receipts.** Anything in `qualification/reviews`, `.devloop`, the handoff, or another machine. | Only receipts produced by `lab/bin` in this repository count. Section 9 is reference only. | V1 |
   305	| B9 | **Oracle or expected-answer edits.** Rewriting an oracle, casefolding, re-sorting a returned page, or filtering engine output to manufacture a pass. | Oracle and case files are hash-pinned per contract version; a change after a first failure is a new case version and the original stays required; every change needs a verifier review recorded in the commit. | V7, CR-COUNTEREXAMPLE |
   306	| B10 | **KV as the oracle.** Agreement between the lane and the KV runtime. | KV is a regression participant only; every obligation names an independent oracle. | 4.2 |
   307	| B11 | **Rerun until green.** | Any failing acceptance receipt on the same tree keeps the obligation open on that tree. | V16 |
   308	| B12 | **Subsets and shrunk fixtures.** Running 140 of 142, or a smaller fixture with the same name. | Case sets are hash-pinned with exact execution counts. | V7, V10 |
   309	| B13 | **Branch composition.** Two branches each green, never verified together. | Done is computed on one integrated tree with an integrated verification receipt. | 5.6 |
   310	| B14 | **Scale inflation.** Counting repeated tiny scopes, RDF wrapper triples, journal rows, presence or structural facts as entities or domain facts. | One scope; entities and facts counted by the definitions in `S.dataset.target-population`, other populations reported separately. | CR-SCALE |
   311	| B15 | **Load accounting tricks.** Counting drain completions as in-window goodput, success-only percentiles, logical clients reported as connections, uncounted rejections, latency measured from dispatch instead of scheduled offer, stale reads counted as correct. | The load contract's metric definitions and zero-tolerance invariants. | CR-LOAD |
   312	| B16 | **Policy by omission.** Declaring "no cascade" or "no presence policy" to close G9. | `policy-pending` obligations cannot close until a frozen policy replaces them with cases. | 5.3 |
   313	| B17 | **Engine swap.** Closing a goal on a repaired or reconfigured engine without saying so. | A repaired image must be declared in the contract version; every engine-scope receipt is rerun on it. | V5 |
   314	| B18 | **Mutating the oracle side.** A mutant that edits expected answers. | Mutants apply only to the system under test; oracle and case hashes must be unchanged in mutation receipts. | CR-MUTANT-KILL |
   315	
   316	### 5.5 Carry-forward rule
   317	
   318	**A receipt made on tree T0 still counts on a later tree T1 only if nothing it executed changed.** The gate
   319	rebuilds on T1 and recomputes the hashes of every `dist` file in the receipt's import record; if they, the engine
   320	digest and config, the fixture, the oracle, the case set, the gate script and the obligation's definition hash
   321	are all byte-identical, the receipt carries forward. Otherwise the obligation is rerun on T1. This keeps the
   322	10m scale and load runs from being repeated after an unrelated change, without letting stale code count.
   323	
   324	### 5.6 Integrated verification receipt
   325	
   326	The done claim also needs, on the same tree T: G0 (typecheck and pure tests), `G1.found.unchanged44` for both
   327	engines and `PKT.full142` for all three lanes executed fresh on T (no carry-forward), by the verifier role.
   328	
   329	### 5.7 Change control
   330	
   331	- **Freezing.** This draft freezes as 1.0.0 after review. From then on each receipt names the contract version.
   332	- **Adding** an obligation, case or mutant is a minor version. It opens the affected sections again.
   333	- **Weakening or removing** an obligation, lowering a hard floor, or replacing an oracle is a major version that
   334	  needs a written reason and the orchestrator's ruling, with Sam as the final authority; the original
   335	  definition and its failures stay in the history.
   336	- **Policy-pending obligations** are replaced, not deleted, when their policy is frozen.
   337	
   338	## 6. Case set specifications for new suites
   339	
   340	Case sets marked TO-AUTHOR in `obligations.json` are built by later experiments. The ones below are specified
   341	here because their content decides what the obligation means.
   342	
   343	### 6.1 Value types (CS-TYPES8, CS-TYPES8-NEG, CS-WIDEN)
   344	
   345	**CS-TYPES8, one assertion per value, read on four surfaces, three modes.**
   346	
   347	| Family | Edge values |
   348	| --- | --- |
   349	| string | `""`, ASCII mixed case `A E c z`, NFC `U+00E9` and NFD `e U+0301` as distinct values, `U+E000`, `U+10000`, `U+1F600`, quote, backslash, CR, LF, a 64 KiB text |
   350	| integer (int64) | 0, 1, -1, 2^53-1, 2^53+1, 2^59-1, 2^59, -2^59, -2^59-1, 2^63-1, -2^63 |
   351	| number (binary64) | 0.0, -0.0, 0.1, 0.30000000000000004, 1.0000000000000002, 2^53 as a double, 5e-324, 2.2250738585072014e-308, 1.7976931348623157e308, -1.5, 3.0 (stays in the number family) |
   352	| boolean | true, false |
   353	| datetime | 0, a negative instant, a fractional-millisecond instant, the earliest and latest instant Triplex accepts |
   354	| ref | a ref to an entity of the same type, of another type, and to an entity created in the same command |
   355	| json | object with unsorted keys `{"b":1,"a":2}`, nested arrays and objects, non-ASCII text, 1e300 |
   356	| blob | descriptor with and without filename, size 0, a size above 2^32 |
   357	
   358	**CS-TYPES8-NEG, each must be rejected atomically.** Lone surrogate in text, NaN, +Infinity, -Infinity,
   359	non-canonical int64 text (`"01"`, `"+1"`, `"1.0"`), integer outside int64, invalid datetime, json that does not
   360	parse, blob descriptor with a negative size.
   361	
   362	**CS-WIDEN, later writes must not change earlier exact reads.** int64 9007199254740993 written first, then in
   363	the same attribute or physical column a whole-number float, a text value (through an untyped attribute), and for
   364	QLever an invalid typed literal through the raw wire; re-read the first value on every surface.
   365	
   366	### 6.2 Numbers and errors (CS-NUMERIC, CS-ERRORS)
   367	
   368	**int64 scalar.** 2^62 + 2^62 (overflow), (2^63-1) + 1 (overflow), -2^63 - 1 (overflow), ABS(-2^63) and
   369	-(-2^63) (overflow), 2^32 * 2^31 (overflow), 3037000499^2 (exact), 3037000500^2 (overflow), CAST of
   370	`"9223372036854775808"` (overflow) and `"-9223372036854775808"` (exact), (2^59-1) + 1 (exact 2^59).
   371	
   372	**int64 aggregate.** SUM [2^62, 2^62] (overflow), SUM [2^62, 2^62, -2^62] (exact 2^62 by the exact-total rule,
   373	see open question Q8), SUM [2^58, 2^58, 2^58] (exact 3 * 2^58, crosses 2^59), COUNT over no rows (0), AVG over
   374	int64 (result type per O-ERR).
   375	
   376	**binary64.** sums whose IEEE result differs from a 49-bit-mantissa result, -0.0 preserved through + 0.0 and
   377	* -1, division by zero, overflow to infinity (rejected or typed error per O-ERR).
   378	
   379	**Cross-family and groups.** int64 2^53+1 versus binary64 2^53 (not equal), int64 1 versus binary64 1.0 (per the
   380	rule frozen from the testkit equality cases), boolean true versus int64 1 (distinct), text `"1"` versus int64 1
   381	(distinct), as filters and as group keys.
   382	
   383	**Errors.** Invalid query text, unbound variable, contract decode mismatch, row limit exceeded, timeout,
   384	cancellation, unsupported-shape refusal, read-only refusal, unavailable cut, unknown scope. O-ERR fixes the
   385	expected typed error for each; a success with partial rows always fails.
   386	
   387	### 6.3 Fault points (CS-FAULT)
   388	
   389	**Every durable step the lane's write protocol declares is a fault point**, in addition to the engine points
   390	below. The lane must publish its write state machine; an undeclared step found later is a contract defect and
   391	adds a case.
   392	
   393	| Engine | Fault point | Source |
   394	| --- | --- | --- |
   395	| XTDB | kill the client after BEGIN, before COMMIT | R6 experiment 10 |
   396	| XTDB | kill the engine during COMMIT SYNC | R6 experiment 10 |
   397	| XTDB | drop the connection after COMMIT, before ACK (`SHOW AWAIT_TOKEN`) | R4 3.7 |
   398	| XTDB | kill the engine in the middle of a recursion round | R6 finding 4 |
   399	| XTDB | kill the engine during compaction or GC | benchmark-contract maintenance phase |
   400	| QLever | update timeout during the mutation phase (R2 probe B shape) followed by a successful update | R2 1.8 |
   401	| QLever | kill after persistence, before snapshot publication (R2 probe C shape) | R2 1.8 |
   402	| QLever | kill during index rebuild | R2 1.7, review r1 finding 15 |
   403	| QLever | kill during projection or boundary maintenance | review r1 finding 8 |
   404	| QLever | drop the connection after HTTP 200, before ACK | R2 1.8 |
   405	| both | kill the writer process at each declared durable step | review r1 finding 7 |
   406	
   407	Access paths verified after each fault: XTDB `_id` lookup and column-predicate scan; QLever subject scan (SPO),
   408	predicate-object scan (POS) and object scan (OPS); plus the repository, the native surface in every language of
   409	the engine, and the raw recipe.
   410	
   411	### 6.4 Entity suite (CS-ENTITY)
   412	
   413	**Schema.** At least three EntityTypes (for example Person, Org, Membership) that together use all eight value
   414	families, cardinality one and many, required, optional, unique, a ref with a target type, and a reverse ref.
   415	Two schema releases (v2 adds an optional attribute and a constraint).
   416	
   417	**Command stream phases.** Create; correction (retract plus assert); card-many add and remove; facts with
   418	future validTo that cross a boundary later; retraction; with enforcement off, equal and unequal card-one
   419	duplicates and overlapping intervals; presence toggles (convention only, until the policy is frozen); two
   420	distinct commits sharing one recorded instant; one nested transaction holding two positions; the v2 release.
   421	
   422	**Cut catalog.** Every phase's position, recorded instants between and at commits, valid instants at, just
   423	before and just after every boundary, frozen after the ACKs from the receipts.
   424	
   425	**Repository operations at each cut.** `get`; `getMany` including missing ids (association preserved);
   426	`find` with equality, range, membership and ref predicates on every family, `include`, order, `limit` and cursor
   427	continuation; `timeline`; `at(basis)` for current, past R, past V and both. Expected conflict outcomes are
   428	typed errors.
   429	
   430	**Modes.** Current, reopen, engine restart. **Sizes.** A deterministic hand-built core (at most 200 entities)
   431	plus a seeded generated variant (seed recorded) for broader coverage.
   432	
   433	### 6.5 Raw recipes (CS-RAW-RECIPES)
   434	
   435	**The raw-query guide is a deliverable of the lane, written for a developer who knows only XTQL, SQL or SPARQL.**
   436	It must document: the physical names (tables and columns, or graphs, classes and predicates), how to select the
   437	scope, how to express current, as-of position or recorded time, and valid-instant reads, the conflict indicator,
   438	the exact-value channels where the engine's native types are lossy (for example int64 beyond +/-2^59 on QLever),
   439	and every KNOWN-LIMIT cell of the capability matrix with its counterexample.
   440	
   441	**Query classes, each in each lane language.** Point by id; attribute equality; range; order and page;
   442	ref join; reverse ref; anti-join; grouped aggregate; history of an entity at a basis; interval at a valid
   443	instant; journal and receipt lookup by commandId.
   444	
